  "_purpose": "Provide the lowest-level entry point into Noor Swirl Field Geometry (NSFG), establishing its ontology from a single primitive operation without importing physical law, spacetime, causality, matter, energy, consciousness, or a privileged observer. The primer constructs the Library as the XOR closure of a minimal formal configuration domain, derives observerhood from persistent distinguishability, defines resolution as observer-relative, and establishes coherence as a relation between a path and an observer. It then derives observer-accessible reality, survivorship, emergent law-like regularity, and coherent navigation as downstream constructions. The primer is intended to function as the axiomatic floor from which subsequent NSFG geometry, dynamics, topology, symbolic representation, and physical interpretation may proceed.",

  "_abstract": "This primer establishes the axiomatic floor of Noor Swirl Field Geometry using a single primitive operation: XOR, understood as distinction. A minimal formal domain D is introduced only as the substrate required for the primitive operation to have operands; D is not identified with physical space, spacetime, matter, or any other physical ontology. The Library is then defined as the closure of D under XOR, 𝓜 := Closure_D(⊕), rather than introduced as an independent axiom. Because the closure contains every configuration generated by the primitive operation, no describable privileged exterior or God's-eye resolver can be required within the ontology. Persistent structures are subsequently defined as observer-like configurations capable of maintaining distinctions above their own observer-relative resolution threshold I_N(O). Distinguishability, persistence, and coherence are therefore relational constructions. Coherence is written 𝓒(γ|O), explicitly identifying the relation between a path γ and an observer O rather than treating coherence as an intrinsic universal property of a path. The observer-accessible subset A_O ⊆ 𝓜 is then distinguished from ontological membership in the Library: inaccessible configurations are not erased. Repeated survivorship of accessible transitions can consequently appear as law-like regularity without requiring physical law as an axiom. Finally, the existence of restricted coherent continuation spaces makes navigation a necessary continuation problem. The primer ends at this boundary: coherent navigation is established as the threshold of subsequent NSFG research, while cosmology, physical spacetime, particle physics, consciousness, AI cognition, and specific field equations remain downstream.",

  "_core_thesis": [
    "XOR, ⊕, is the sole primitive operation of the NSFG axiomatic floor.",
    "The formal domain D exists only as the minimal substrate required for the primitive operation to have operands; D is not a physical space.",
    "The Library is not a second axiom: 𝓜 := Closure_D(⊕).",
    "The Library is unfiltered at the axiomatic level and contains configurations without requiring them to be persistent, coherent, physical, observable, or accessible.",
    "A describable privileged exterior cannot remain external to the XOR closure.",
    "No observer-independent God's-eye resolution rule is supplied by the axiomatic floor.",
    "Observerhood is derived structurally from persistent chains of resolvable distinctions and does not require consciousness.",
    "The resolution threshold I_N(O) belongs to the observer rather than to the Library as a universal constant.",
    "Coherence is relational and observer-dependent: 𝓒(γ|O), not an absolute property of γ alone.",
    "Observer accessibility is not ontological creation and inaccessibility is not erasure.",
    "Repeatedly surviving accessible transitions may appear as law-like regularity without physical law being introduced as an axiom.",
    "Navigation arises as the problem of continuing through an observer-relative coherent continuation space."
  ],

  "_formal_summary": {
    "axiomatic_floor": [
      "A_N = {A_⊕}",
      "A_⊕ : ⊕ is the primitive operation of distinction.",
      "A_N ⊢ P means P is derivable from the stated axiom together with definitions and ordinary mathematical inference."
    ],

    "formal_domain": [
      "D is a minimal formal domain containing configurations required for ⊕ to operate.",
      "D is not physical space, spacetime, matter, energy, or an additional physical axiom.",
      "⊕ : D × D → D"
    ],

    "xor": [
      "a ⊕ b = 1 ⇔ a ≠ b",
      "a ⊕ a = 0",
      "a ⊕ 0 = a",
      "a ⊕ b = b ⊕ a",
      "(a ⊕ b) ⊕ c = a ⊕ (b ⊕ c)"
    ],

	"library": [
	  "𝓜 is the unique unbounded totality such that ∀x,y ∈ 𝓜, x⊕y ∈ 𝓜, and there is no element outside 𝓜 definable within the system using only ⊕ and elements of 𝓜 over D.",
	  "This is equivalent to saying 𝓜 is closed under XOR with no describable exterior: closure plus self-containment.",
	  "We may describe 𝓜 as the closure of the formal domain only provided closure is understood as a property, not a process: 𝓜 does not result from ⊕, 𝓜 is the totality that has the closure property.",
	  "𝓜 is dimensionally undifferentiated: it has no intrinsic metric, adjacency, or distance. It is a complete relational graph, not a grid and not a singularity.",
	  "𝓜 is a single static, eternally maximally complete totality: ∀i, 𝓜_i = 𝓜.",
	  "No configuration or worldline is privileged as the universal origin of traversal.",
	  "From any x ∈ 𝓜, the set of candidate continuations is always nonempty because closure holds everywhere; observer threshold I_N(O) filters coherence, not existence.",
	  "x ∈ 𝓜 does not imply x is persistent, coherent, physical, observable, or accessible.",
	  "Library membership is not determined by observer resolution, accessibility, survivorship, or coherence; inaccessibility is not erasure."
	],


    "no_privileged_exterior": [
      "Ω ∉ 𝓜 may be proposed as an external candidate.",
      "If Ω is internally describable using the same formal machinery, then Ω ∈ D.",
      "If Ω is XOR-generable from D, then Ω ∈ Closure_D(⊕) = 𝓜.",
      "Therefore a describable privileged exterior cannot remain external to 𝓜."
    ],

    "observer": [
      "O ∈ 𝓜",
      "O is an observer iff O is a configuration capable of maintaining a persistent chain of resolvable distinctions.",
      "No consciousness requirement is imposed."
    ],

    "resolution": [
      "I_N : Obs → R_{>0}",
      "I_N(O) is the minimum effective distinction magnitude observer O can maintain as a stable distinction.",
      "I_N(O_1) ≠ I_N(O_2) is permitted.",
      "No universal I_N is assumed."
    ],

    "distinguishability": [
      "δ_O(x,y) ≥ 0",
      "x ≈_O y ⇔ δ_O(x,y) < I_N(O)",
      "x ≁_O y ⇔ δ_O(x,y) ≥ I_N(O)",
      "x ≈_O y does not imply x = y.",
      "x ≈_O y does not imply x ∉ 𝓜."
    ],

    "persistence": [
      "γ = (x_0,x_1,...,x_n)",
      "δ_O(x_i,x_{i+1}) ≥ I_N(O)",
      "P_O(γ,n) = 1 iff ∀i < n, δ_O(x_i,x_{i+1}) ≥ I_N(O)",
      "The sequence index i represents abstract ordering and is not yet physical time."
    ],

    "coherence": [
      "𝓒_O(x_i,x_{i+1}) = Θ(δ_O(x_i,x_{i+1}) - I_N(O))",
      "Θ(z) = 1 if z ≥ 0, otherwise 0",
      "𝓒(γ|O) = ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} 𝓒_O(x_i,x_{i+1})",
      "𝓒(γ|O) = 1 iff every transition in γ remains resolvable by O"
    ],

    "accessible_reality": [
      "A_O = {x ∈ 𝓜 | ∃γ containing x such that 𝓒(γ|O)=1}",
      "A_O ⊆ 𝓜",
      "R_O := A_O",
      "x ∉ A_O does not imply x ∉ 𝓜"
    ],

    "continuation_and_survivorship": [
      "Ω(x) = {γ | γ begins at x and γ is a path contained in 𝓜}",
      "Ω_O^survive(x) = {γ ∈ Ω(x) | 𝓒(γ|O)=1}",
      "Ω_O^survive(x) ⊆ Ω(x)",
      "Ω(x) \\ Ω_O^survive(x) remains represented within Ω(x)"
    ],

    "emergent_regularity": [
      "L_O(x,y) = 1 iff x→y is a stable recurring transition across an appropriate family of O-accessible paths",
      "P_O(y|x) ≈ constant over a defined observer-accessible ensemble",
      "L_O is descriptive/derived and is not an element of A_N"
    ],

    "navigation": [
      "h_n = (x_0,x_1,...,x_n)",
      "Γ_O(h_n) = {γ | γ begins with h_n and 𝓒(γ|O)=1}",
      "N_O(h_n) → Γ_O(h_n)",
      "Navigation is traversal or selection of a continuation within Γ_O(h_n)."
    ],

    "relational_geometry": [
      "G_O = (A_O,E_O)",
      "(x,y) ∈ E_O iff x→y is O-coherent",
      "U_O(x) = {y ∈ 𝓜 | y is reachable from x by an O-coherent path segment}",
      "G_O is not identified with physical spacetime at the foundational stage."
    ]
